= Solution 5: Statistics and Climate Indices
<solution-5-statistics-and-climate-indices>
== Task 1: Basic Statistics
<task-1-basic-statistics>
=== Commands:
<commands>
```bash
cdo timmean ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timmean.nc
cdo timstd ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timstd.nc
cdo timmin ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timmin.nc
cdo timmax ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timmax.nc
cdo timpctl,10 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timmin.nc tas_timmax.nc tas_p10.nc
cdo timpctl,50 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timmin.nc tas_timmax.nc tas_p50.nc
cdo timpctl,90 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_timmin.nc tas_timmax.nc tas_p90.nc
```

=== Answers:
<answers>
+ #strong[Three inputs for `timpctl`:]
  - Input data field (the dataset)
  - Minimum field (`timmin` --- the minimum value at each grid point)
  - Maximum field (`timmax` --- the maximum value at each grid point)
  - The min/max are needed as bounds for the percentile calculation
    algorithm.
+ #strong[Median operator:] The median is the 50th percentile:
  `cdo timpctl,50 input.nc min.nc max.nc median.nc`
+ #strong[Spatial percentiles:] Use `fldpctl` (spatial percentile across
  grid cells): `cdo fldpctl,90 input.nc`
+ #strong[`timmean` vs `fldmean`:]
  - `timmean`: average #strong[over time] at each grid point → spatial
    field
  - `fldmean`: average #strong[over space] at each time step → time
    series

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

#figure(image("../../assets/sol05_task1_basic_stats.png"),
  caption: [
    Time-mean temperature distribution with the 10th, 50th and 90th
    percentiles, and the global mean time series.
  ]
)

== Task 2: Frost Days and Tropical Nights
<task-2-frost-days-and-tropical-nights>
=== Commands:
<commands-1>
```bash
cdo lt,273.15 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_below_0C.nc
cdo yearsum tas_below_0C.nc tas_frost_months.nc
cdo gt,293.15 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc tas_above_20C.nc
cdo yearsum tas_above_20C.nc tas_tropical_months.nc
```

=== Answers:
<answers-1>
+ #strong[Monthly data bias for frost days:] Monthly means smooth out
  sub-monthly variability. A month with both frost and thaw days can
  have a mean above 0°C, so frost days would be underestimated.
+ #strong[More accurate frost days from monthly data:] Use diurnal
  temperature range or minimum temperature if available (`tasmin`). With
  only `tas`, apply a statistical correction or use a higher threshold
  (e.g., monthly mean \< 1°C as proxy).
+ #strong[Operator for consecutive threshold exceedance:] `consects`
  (consecutive streaks of true values in a binary field).
+ #strong[Standard temperature thresholds:]
  - #strong[Ice days:] `tasmax < 0°C` (273.15 K) --- daily maximum below
    freezing
  - #strong[Summer days:] `tasmax > 25°C` (298.15 K) --- daily maximum
    above 25°C
  - #strong[Growing season length:] Number of days with `tas > 5°C`
    (278.15 K) for at least 6 consecutive days

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

#figure(image("../../assets/sol05_task2_indices.png"),
  caption: [
    Counts of frost months (tas \< 0 °C) and warm months (tas \> 20 °C)
    from real reanalysis data.
  ]
)

== Task 3: Consecutive Dry Days (CDD) and Consecutive Wet Days (CWD)
<task-3-consecutive-dry-days-cdd-and-consecutive-wet-days-cwd>
=== Commands:
<commands-2>
```bash
cdo lt,1.0 ../datasets/pr_sample.nc pr_dry.nc
cdo ge,1.0 ../datasets/pr_sample.nc pr_wet.nc
cdo consects pr_dry.nc pr_cdd.nc
cdo consects pr_wet.nc pr_cwd.nc
```

=== Answers:
<answers-2>
+ #strong[What `consects` computes:] For each grid cell, it computes the
  length of the longest consecutive sequence of `true` (1) values in the
  binary mask. For dry days, it's the longest dry spell.
+ #strong[Maximum CDD per year:] `cdo yearmax pr_cdd.nc`
+ #strong[Typical wet day threshold:] 1 mm/day is the most common. The
  IPCC uses 1 mm/day for most indices. Some studies use 0.1 mm/day for
  very arid regions.
+ #strong[0.1 vs 1.0 mm/day threshold:] A lower threshold (0.1 mm/day)
  produces longer wet spells and shorter dry spells. CDD decreases, CWD
  increases.
+ #strong[Dry vs wet spell comparison:]
  - Dry spells (CDD) tend to be longer, especially in arid regions and
    dry seasons
  - Wet spells (CWD) are typically shorter, often 3--7 days in most
    climates
  - The difference is most pronounced in monsoon and Mediterranean
    climates

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

#figure(image("../../assets/sol05_task3_cdd_cwd.png"),
  caption: [
    Longest consecutive dry and wet spells per year from real CHIRPS
    monthly precipitation.
  ]
)

== Task 4: Create Histograms of Precipitation
<task-4-create-histograms-of-precipitation>
=== Python code:
<python-code>
```python
import xarray as xr; import matplotlib.pyplot as plt; import numpy as np
ds = xr.open_dataset('../datasets/pr_sample.nc')
pr = ds['pr'].values.flatten()
pr = pr[~np.isnan(pr)]
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
plt.hist(pr, bins=50, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white', alpha=0.7)
plt.xlabel('Precipitation (mm/day)'); plt.ylabel('Frequency')
plt.title('Histogram of Monthly Precipitation (Global)')
plt.grid(alpha=0.3)
plt.axvline(np.mean(pr), color='red', ls='--', label=f'Mean: {np.mean(pr):.2f}')
plt.axvline(np.median(pr), color='green', ls='--', label=f'Median: {np.median(pr):.2f}')
plt.legend(); plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig('precip_histogram.png', dpi=150)
```

=== Answers:
<answers-3>
+ #strong[Positive skew of precipitation:] Precipitation is bounded at
  zero (cannot be negative) and has a long tail of high values from
  extreme rainfall events. Most days/months have little or no
  precipitation, while a few have very high amounts.
+ #strong[Fraction below 1 mm/day:] Typically 60--80% of global
  grid-month values are below 1 mm/day, reflecting the large dry areas
  (deserts, polar regions) and dry seasons.
+ #strong[95th percentile:] (Will vary by dataset, typically 5--15
  mm/day for monthly data). Compute with: `np.percentile(pr, 95)`
+ #strong[Tropical vs mid-latitude distributions:]
  - #strong[Tropics:] Higher mean, narrower distribution (wet year-round
    in many areas)
  - #strong[Mid-latitudes:] Lower mean but wider distribution (distinct
    wet/dry seasons)
  - #strong[Subtropics:] Highly skewed with many dry values and few wet
    events

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

#figure(image("../../assets/sol05_task4_histograms.png"),
  caption: [
    Histogram of real CHIRPS monthly precipitation with mean, median and
    95th percentile marked.
  ]
)

== Bonus Challenge: Standardized Precipitation Index (SPI)
<bonus-challenge-standardized-precipitation-index-spi>
=== Commands:
<commands-3>
```bash
cdo runsum,3 -monsum ../datasets/pr_sample.nc pr_runsum3.nc
cdo sub pr_runsum3.nc -timmean pr_runsum3.nc pr_spi_anom.nc
cdo div pr_spi_anom.nc -timstd pr_runsum3.nc pr_spi3.nc
```

=== Drought month count:
<drought-month-count>
```bash
cdo lt,-1.0 pr_spi3.nc pr_drought_mask.nc
cdo fldmean -timsum pr_drought_mask.nc
```

=== SPI classification:
<spi-classification>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 2,
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([SPI Value], [Category],),
    table.hline(),
    [\> 2.0], [Extremely wet],
    [1.5 to 2.0], [Severely wet],
    [1.0 to 1.5], [Moderately wet],
    [-1.0 to 1.0], [Near normal],
    [-1.5 to -1.0], [Moderately dry],
    [-2.0 to -1.5], [Severely dry],
    [\< -2.0], [Extremely dry],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

=== Python plot for a grid cell:
<python-plot-for-a-grid-cell>
```python
ds = xr.open_dataset('pr_spi3.nc')
spi = ds['pr'].isel(lat=45, lon=0)
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 3))
spi.plot(color='steelblue')
plt.axhline(-1, color='orange', ls='--')
plt.axhline(-2, color='red', ls='--')
plt.axhline(1, color='orange', ls='--')
plt.axhline(2, color='red', ls='--')
plt.title('SPI-3 at (0°E, 45°N)')
plt.tight_layout(); plt.savefig('spi3_timeseries.png', dpi=150)
```

#figure(image("../../assets/sol05_bonus_spi.png"),
  caption: [
    SPI-3 at approximately 9°N, 40°E with drought and wet-category
    thresholds, from real CHIRPS data.
  ]
)
